From: Steve Shoecraft <spshoecraft@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drbd: do not sleep in an RCU read-side section when a fenced
 peer is outdated

When the fence-peer handler of a Primary running "fencing
resource-and-stonith" succeeds (exit code 4 or 7), w_after_conn_state_ch()
creates the new current UUID of each volume of the connection while it walks
connection->peer_devices under rcu_read_lock().  drbd_uuid_new_current()
writes the meta data and waits for that write, so the worker sleeps inside
the RCU read-side critical section:

  Voluntary context switch within RCU read-side critical section!
  WARNING: CPU: 0 PID: 27578 at kernel/rcu/tree_plugin.h:332 rcu_note_context_switch+0x532/0x5a0
  ...
   wait_until_done_or_force_detached
   _drbd_md_sync_page_io
   drbd_md_write
   drbd_md_sync
   drbd_uuid_new_current
   w_after_conn_state_ch
   drbd_worker

It happens on every successful fencing of a peer by a Primary; seen on
6.8 (Ubuntu 24.04) and 6.17 (Proxmox VE 9) with the in-tree 8.4.11 driver.

Take a reference on the device and leave the RCU read-side section around
the UUID update, as conn_md_sync() already does for the same walk.

---
The Signed-off-by line is the submitter's to add.

 drivers/block/drbd/drbd_state.c | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/block/drbd/drbd_state.c b/drivers/block/drbd/drbd_state.c
--- a/drivers/block/drbd/drbd_state.c
+++ b/drivers/block/drbd/drbd_state.c
@@ -2090,8 +2090,12 @@ static int w_after_conn_state_ch(struct drbd_work *w, int unused)
 			idr_for_each_entry(&connection->peer_devices, peer_device, vnr) {
 				struct drbd_device *device = peer_device->device;
 				if (test_bit(NEW_CUR_UUID, &device->flags)) {
+					kref_get(&device->kref);
+					rcu_read_unlock();
 					drbd_uuid_new_current(device);
 					clear_bit(NEW_CUR_UUID, &device->flags);
+					kref_put(&device->kref, drbd_destroy_device);
+					rcu_read_lock();
 				}
 			}
 			rcu_read_unlock();
